<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Grigory Potemkin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1739">1739</a>–<a href="/years/1791">1791</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Prince Potemkin, Grigory Aleksandrovich Potemkin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Grigory Potemkin was a Russian statesman and field marshal, the favourite and close partner in government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine the Great</a>. As governor of New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> he directed the annexation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a> in 1783, founded towns such as Kherson and Mykolaiv, and built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea-fleet">Black Sea Fleet</a>. He commanded Russian forces in the Russo-Turkish War of 1787–1792, taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ochakiv">Ochakiv</a> in 1788, and died during the peace talks in 1791.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/grigory-potemkin.jpg" alt="Grigory Potemkin" width="320" height="448" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: After Johann Baptist von Lampi the Elder · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Princepotemkin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Grigory Potemkin was born on 11 October 1739 (30 September in the old style) in the village of Chizhovo near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smolensk">Smolensk</a>, into a family of middle-income noble landowners. After his father's death in 1746 his mother, Daria Vasilievna Kondyreva, took charge of the family and moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, where he studied at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gymnasium">gymnasium</a> attached to the university. He enlisted in the army in 1750 at the age of eleven, as noble children customarily did, and won the university's gold medal in 1757. He later lost the sight of his left eye, under circumstances the sources tell differently.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Potemkin returned to court in January 1774, and by February had become Catherine's lover and favourite. The offices that followed included Governor-General of Novorossiya, General-in-Chief and Commander-in-Chief of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossacks</a>. Their surviving letters show a relationship of laughter and mutual admiration. He pursued the Greek Project, which aimed at a new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, and by July 1783 he had engineered the peaceful annexation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a> and Kuban. Catherine rewarded him with one hundred thousand roubles, which he used to build the Tauride Palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a>.</p><p>He became a field marshal in 1784 and governed the southern provinces as Prince of Taurida. His firm dealing with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossacks</a>, notably the breaking of the Zaporozhian host in 1775, defined his rule. He founded or developed cities: Kherson, begun in 1778 as a base for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea-fleet">Black Sea Fleet</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sevastopol">Sevastopol</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simferopol">Simferopol</a>; and Nikolayev, founded in 1789. His effort to build Yekaterinoslav, now Dnipro, was his biggest failure. When a second Russo-Turkish war began in August 1787 he was commander-in-chief, and his army besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ochakiv">Ochakov</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Potemkin fell ill at Iași, where he oversaw peace talks, and died on 16 October 1791 (5 October in the old style) on the open steppe, 60 km from the town. Some historians have suggested poisoning, but Simon Sebag Montefiore proposes bronchial pneumonia. Catherine bought the Tauride Palace and his art collection and paid his debts. When Paul I succeeded in 1796 he tried to undo Potemkin's reforms. The Potemkin village, a ruse of painted façades to impress visiting officials, is associated with his rule but is probably mythical. The battleship Potemkin, named in his honour, took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-revolution-of-1905">Russian Revolution of 1905</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Grigory Potemkin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Advised by <b>Grigory Potemkin</b>, Catherine II proclaimed the annexation of Crimea to the Russian Empire on 8 April 1783, and Şahin Giray abdicated.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crimean-khanate">Crimean Khanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1787, on the eve of war with the Ottoman Empire, Prince <b>Grigory Potemkin</b> gathered former Zaporozhian Cossacks into the Host of Loyal Zaporozhians.</blockquote><a href="/state/black-sea-cossack-host">Black Sea Cossack Host</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Chizhevo, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. Died in Rădenii Vechi, in what is now <a href="/countries/moldova">Moldova</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=55.15,32.17&amp;year=1772" aria-label="Chizhevo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Grigory Potemkin"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5106 5413h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5106 5413h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=55.15,32.17&amp;year=1772">What was here in 1772? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
